  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Zinza\"\nsubtitle: \"Tanzania\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Zinza, a Bantu people of northwestern Tanzania |\n| Where | Southwestern islands of Lake Victoria and adjacent lakeshore in Geita and Mwanza Regions |\n| How many | About 138,000 (estimated 1987) |\n| Language | Zinza (Luzinza), a Bantu language |\n| Religion | Not documented |\n| Known for | Black-and-white plaited grass basketry · Twilled head cushions · Incised calabash vessels |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Zinza are a Bantu ethnolinguistic group living on the southwestern islands of Lake Victoria and the lakeshore of northwestern Tanzania. Their language, Zinza (also called Luzinza), has been documented through lexicons and grammatical studies. The primary material record comes from British Museum objects registered in 1950: plaited grass containers, head cushions, decorated calabashes, and smoking pipes.\n\n## Material culture\n\nZinza material culture survives chiefly through plaited grass work in black and white, decorated gourds, and practical objects for storage and personal use.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe Zinza's clearest design tradition is black-and-white plaited grass work, with patterning carried in the plaiting itself.\n\n- **Black-and-white plaited grass baskets** — Grass baskets patterned in black and white with matching plaited lids, where color is created through the plaiting technique itself.\n- **Twilled head cushion** — A circular plaited head-ring in black and white worked with twilled plait to create a diagonally striped effect.\n- **Incised calabash ornament** — A yellow calabash decorated with fine burnt incision on the gourd wall.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nNo information about Zinza dress, garments, or body coverings is recorded in available sources.\n\n### Architecture\n\nNo descriptions of Zinza houses, building materials, or settlement patterns exist in the sources.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nZinza everyday objects include containers, carrying equipment, and smoking implements.\n\n- **Plaited grass containers** — Two grass baskets with a plaited lid, all patterned in black and white, registered in 1950.\n- **Head-ring** — A circular grass head cushion in twilled plait used as a pad to carry loads on the head.\n- **Calabash vessel** — A yellow gourd vessel with fine incised, burnt ornament.\n- **Soapstone tobacco pipe** — A smoking pipe with a wooden stem and black soapstone bowl bound with copper wire.\n- **Spotted snuff bottle** — A snuff bottle ornamented with spots, showing use of both snuff and smoked tobacco.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nNo jewelry, beadwork, or personal adornment practices are documented for the Zinza.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nNo information on Zinza musical instruments or performance exists in available sources.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nNo Zinza dances, masquerades, or theatrical traditions are recorded.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nNo Zinza festivals, life-cycle rites, or ceremonial practices are documented in available sources.\n\n## Foodways\n\nZinza cooking, staples, and dining customs are not described, though storage and consumption objects survive.\n\n- **Plaited grass containers** — Baskets and lids used for food storage or carrying.\n- **Calabash vessel** — A gourd container, possibly for food or drink storage.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nNo folktales, epics, or proverbs are recorded; the main preservation effort is in lexicographic and grammatical documentation of the language.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nZinza is a Bantu language spoken on the southern shore of Lake Victoria, documented in lexicons and grammatical studies; no religious practices are recorded.\n\n- **Luzinza** — The language's own name for itself, a Bantu language of Tanzania.\n- **Wazinza** — The Swahili name for the Zinza people.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Luzinza* — The Zinza language's own name for itself\n- *Wazinza* — The Swahili name for the Zinza people\n- *Zinza* — A Bantu ethnolinguistic group of northwestern Tanzania\n- *plaited grass* — Woven grass technique used for baskets and cushions\n- *twilled plait* — A diagonal-weave plaiting technique creating stripe patterns\n- *calabash* — A yellow gourd vessel used for storage or serving\n- *head-ring* — A circular woven pad placed on the head to carry loads\n- *soapstone* — Black stone used for the bowl of a tobacco pipe\n- *snuff* — Powdered tobacco inhaled through the nose\n- *Bantu* — A major language family of sub-Saharan Africa\n- *Lake Victoria* — The large lake on Tanzania's northwestern border where Zinza live\n- *lexicon* — A comprehensive list of words and their meanings in a language\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Rubanza, Yunus Ismail. Lizinza: msamiati wa Luzinza-Kiswahili-Kiingereza na Kiingereza-Luzinza-Kiswahili. Languages of Tanzania, vol. 8. Dept. of Foreign Languages and Linguistics, University of Dar es Salaam, 2008. ISBN 9789987691104.\n- Rubanza, Yunus Ismail. Luzinza lexicon. Languages of Tanzania, vol. 9. Dept. of Foreign Languages and Linguistics, University of Dar es Salaam, 2008.\n- Odom, Shannon Ronit. The Grammar Basics of Zinza. Dallas, Texas: SIL International, 2016.\n- Kulwa, Bahati. Assessment of Zinza Language Lexical Change: A Case of Kome Island Sengerema District. MA thesis, University of Dodoma, 2016. hdl:20.500.12661/1127.\n- Odden, David. \"The phrasal tonology of Zinza.\" Journal of African Languages and Linguistics 21, no. 1 (2000): 45–76. Walter de Gruyter. doi:10.1515/jall.2000.21.1.45.\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinza_people\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinza_language\n- British Museum online collection (the 1950-registered Zinza baskets, lid, head-ring, calabash, pipe and snuff bottle shown here): https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Tanzania\n\n",
